  "s the statement\narrives attached in the chat, already in front of you.\n\n**A statement is evidence of the past.** Every line on it proves a charge happened, on a\ndate, for an amount. Record what it proves; do not ask permission for it. \"Shall I add\nthis?\" about a charge that already cleared has one answer, and a year of statements asked\nthat way is an interrogation, not an import.\n\n**Ask what the paper cannot say.** A statement carries a payee string, not what the charge\nwas, who it was for, or whether it repeats — and those are the questions worth a person's\nattention. Collect them while you classify, ask them in one batch when you have finished\nreading, short enough to answer out loud, and write once. A good one names the evidence and\nasks the unknown: \"₹5,400 to a name I cannot place, tw" +
  "ice since March — what is it, and is\nit for anyone in particular?\"\n\n**Whether it repeats is settled by three kinds of evidence, in order.** The bank's own\nmarker in the narration; the same name at about the same amount three or more times at a\nsteady rhythm; a payee that repeats by nature, seen once or twice. Write the lines first,\neach as a one-time row; `promote_to_recurring` each commitment the evidence makes and show\nit in the table — one backed by fewer than three sightings as a guess, its `name` ending\n\" (Guessed)\" and its `notes` opening \"Guessed: \" and the reason, so the owner sees it for\nwhat it is and renames it to keep it. Where writes are proposals, the rows reach the memory\nonly when the owner's app applies them: list the repeats, and promote them once\n`commitments` shows the " +
  "rows. A promotion keeps every charge the statement proved, exactly\nas written, and plans ahead from the last of them. With nobody in the conversation to\nanswer, do not wait: leave the rows `once` and propose the promotions in your note, per\n`Unattended runs`.\n\nThis is the whole flow:\n\n1. `list_documents` → `read_document` each (long files page through `next_offset`; keep\n   asking until it is null). Credit-card statements hold the card's real charges; the bank\n   statement's \"card bill payment\" line is a line like any other, not a transfer. It is\n   written as the statement prints it, and a charge of the card is written from the card's\n   own statement: if both statements are imported, say so in your report, because the\n   same money then shows on the bank and on the card.\n2. `commitments`" +
  " with status \"all\", so what is already there is known — a line that\n   belongs to a commitment that exists is `add_charges` on it, paid, basis \"statement\",\n   not a twin.\n3. Read every line by the rules below. They are the same words longbook's own reader\n   follows, so a statement read by you and one read by the app agree. Every line is a\n   one-time row at its real date and amount, `paid: true`, `basis: \"statement\"`; its kind\n   decides what you say about it and what you promote afterwards.\n\n   **Reading the lines.**\n\n   - Every dated line with an amount is one charge: never totalled, never skipped for being\n     small, however many there are.\n   - A reversal or a refund is its own line, as printed, never netted against the charge it\n     undoes.\n   - The running balance is never a charg" +
  "e; the closing balance and the day it was true are\n     kept, as printed, and never worked out.\n   - The statement's own columns settle which way the money went before its words do: a line in\n     the credit column, or after which the balance rose, is money in whatever the narration\n     says; a line in the debit column is money out even when the narration reads like a\n     receipt.\n   - Never invent a line, a date or an amount, and never total or convert anything.\n\n   **The kinds.**\n\n   - Every line is one kind. spend: money that bought something, or money moved out. fee: a\n     bank charge, tax or interest paid. for_member: money paid for or to a person in the\n     owner's life, naming the person. income: salary, a refund, cashback, interest received,\n     anything credited. Whether a li" +
  "ne is a transfer is never a kind: the Transfers rule\n     decides it.\n\n   **Transfers.**\n\n   - A charge is a transfer only when both its account and the other side's account belong to a\n     person whose relationship is Self. Nothing else is a transfer: not an ATM withdrawal, a\n     wallet load, a payment through CRED or of a card bill, and not words in the line.\n   - longbook applies this by itself. Setting a person to Self, or an account's owner, makes\n     the matching charges transfers; clearing it returns them to money out or money in as the\n     statement printed them. Write direction \"transfer\" only for a line that meets the rule;\n     any other line is out (no direction) or \"in\".\n\n   **People.**\n\n   - A person's name is written exactly as the line prints it, title-cased, even when " +
  "the bank\n     cut it short: \"HOORNAPRIYA AMBA FUE\" stays \"Hoornapriya Amba Fue\". It is never completed,\n     never shortened further, and never guessed from an amount or a role.\n   - The account holder is the person the statement belongs to, named in its header: written\n     exactly as printed, in holder, and null when the header prints none. The holder is a\n     person in the longbook with the relationship Self, and the statement's own account is\n     theirs. A line's person who shares at least two words with the holder, in any order, is\n     the holder, never a second person; a bank account printed beside that person is the\n     holder's account.\n   - Every person a line names is a member, once, however often seen, whether the money went\n     out or came in.\n   - A merchant, a shop or a " +
  "company is never a person; a line that names no person names no\n     member.\n\n   **The other side.**\n\n   - The other side of a line is named only when the line prints it; otherwise there is none,\n     never a guess.\n   - A bank's IFSC code printed on the line names that bank: UTIB is Axis Bank, BARB Bank of\n     Baroda, HDFC HDFC Bank, ICIC ICICI Bank, SBIN State Bank of India, KKBK Kotak, SURY\n     Suryoday Small Finance Bank, and so on. An account is written as its bank, its kind when\n     printed, and the last four digits after \"••\" (\"Axis savings ••1234\", \"HDFC card ••4417\",\n     \"Paytm wallet\"); never a UPI id, a phone number or a full number.\n   - When a line prints both a person and that person's bank account, the person is the other\n     side and the account is kept beside them as " +
  "the person's bank account.\n   - The owner's own other account, on a payment to their own savings, card or wallet or on a\n     card bill payment, is the other side too, named the same way; it is never left out for\n     being the owner's or for being seen once.\n   - A merchant or a shop is never the other side, and neither is the statement's own account.\n\n   **Names.**\n\n   - A charge's name is what the owner would call it, two to four words: \"Netflix\", \"House\n     rent\", \"Axis card bill\".\n   - A name never carries an amount, a currency sign, a cycle, a card or app name in brackets,\n     or anything in brackets at all.\n   - A person's charge is named by the person, as printed.\n   - When a charge's name is the person's name, it is \"Payment to X\" for money out and \"Payment\n     from X\" for mone" +
  "y in, X being the person. Every repeat naming that person takes the same\n     name.\n\n   **What comes round.**\n\n   - The bank's own marker in the narration (AUTOPAY, MANDATE, SI, NACH, ACH, ECS, STANDING\n     INSTRUCTION, EMI) makes a commitment, even when seen once.\n   - The same name at about the same amount, three or more times at a steady rhythm (monthly\n     about 30 days apart, yearly about 365, quarterly 91, half-yearly 183) makes a commitment.\n   - A payee that repeats by nature, seen once or twice, makes a commitment: insurance or a\n     premium, an EMI or a loan, rent, school or fees, society maintenance, a domain or hosting,\n     telecom, a utility, a salary to a person.\n   - A commitment backed by fewer than three sightings is guessed, with a reason in one\n     sentence naming t" +
  "he evidence and the cycle assumed (\"Seen once with the bank's mandate\n     mark; yearly assumed from the insurer's name\"); three or more at a steady rhythm is not a\n     guess.\n   - The cycle comes from the interval when seen more than once, and from the words otherwise:\n     an EMI, rent, a salary, telecom, a utility and maintenance are monthly; insurance and a\n     domain are yearly unless the amount reads monthly.\n   - A repeat that has stopped ends at its last charge.\n   - Everything else stays a one-time charge; nothing becomes a repeat from its amount alone.\n\n   **Notes.**\n\n   - Every charge's note is \"Statement line: \" followed by the line as printed, with reference,\n     account and card numbers removed.\n   - A guessed commitment's name ends with \" (Guessed)\" and its notes begin wi" +
  "th the line\n     \"Guessed: \" and the reason; the owner's edit of the name removes both.\n   - A commitment's notes hold only what the statement states, as the three details that\n     matter: never a guess, never a \"Paid by\" line, never a reference beyond the statement.\n\n   **Accounts.**\n\n   - The statement's own account is an account in the longbook, named from its header (bank,\n     kind, last four digits), owned by the holder, with its closing balance and the day it was\n     true.\n   - An account's name never carries a person's name: \"HDFC Bank\", with the person as its\n     owner. Two accounts at one bank that print no digits are told apart by their owner, not by\n     their name.\n   - Every account printed on another side of a line, the other side itself or a person's bank\n     account, i" +
  "s an account in the longbook too, owned by the person when the line names one.\n\n   What each kind becomes: spend, fee and for_member are one-time rows, a for_member row\n   with `member` the person as printed; income is the same row with `direction: \"in\"`, kept\n   beside spending and never added to it. No line is skipped for being the owner's own\n   money moving: an ATM withdrawal, a wallet load and a card bill payment are one-time rows\n   like the rest. A line is written with `direction: \"transfer\"` only by the Transfers\n   rule: both its account and the other side's account owned by a person tagged self. Then\n   it is shown on the timeline, never in spending, never in money in. longbook applies the\n   rule itself once the people and the owners are set. If the owner\n   said \"only the commi" +
  "tments\" or \"skip food delivery\", honour that; otherwise every line\n   is a fact, however small.\n4. Model honestly, the way the ledger works (see \"The past is stored\"): every line paid,\n   with `basis: \"statement\"`, at the date and amount on the paper; `ends_on` when the\n   source shows an end; a plan only as far as the owner asked. Each row wears the name the\n   Names rules give it — \"Netflix\", not \"UPI/4521/NETFLIX.COM\" — and its charge's `note`\n   is the statement line, per the Notes rules, with every reference, account and card\n   number removed.\n5. Tags from the library first (the icon then resolves in the app on its own); invent only\n   when nothing fits. A commitment's `notes` hold its three details that matter, one per\n   line, only as far as the statement states them — `Notes` belo" +
  "w lists them by category;\n   a guessed commitment's notes open with the \"Guessed: \" line first.\n6. People and accounts, by the People and Accounts rules: `add_person` every person a line\n   names, as printed, once; `add_account` the statement's own account, and every account\n   printed on another side of a line — the other side itself, or the bank account printed\n   beside a person — with `member` the person when the line names one. The statement's\n   closing balance and date go on its account as `balance` and `balance_date`.\n7. Write, show, promote, ask, correct. One `add_commitments` call (or a few, back to\n   back) with every line; then, when the statement prints a closing balance, one\n   `update_account` on the statement's account — `balance` the closing figure,\n   `balance_date` the s" +
  "tatement's last day — so the account reads as the statement left\n   it; `promote_to_recurring` for each commitment the three kinds of evidence make, the\n   guessed ones named so; the table of what you chose and guessed; one message carrying\n   every question the reading left open — what an unidentifiable charge was, who a person\n   is to the owner — and the corrections in one pass. Then stop and report: how many rows,\n   what was promoted, which of those were guesses and how far each is planned, what you\n   skipped and why, and the balance you set with its day — or that the statement printed\n   none.\n8. Finish the picture if asked: two or three `leave_insight` notes. The reports for the\n   months the statements cover are longbook's to draw, and it draws them.\n\n### Worked lines\n\nA bank stat" +
  "ement, twelve months, the lines that matter and what each becomes:\n\n```\n01/07/2026  NETFLIX.COM                649.00  (and 01/06, 01/05 … 01/08/2025)\n→ twelve one-time rows, one per charge, paid, basis statement, tag streaming. Seen twelve\n  times a month apart and still going: promote_to_recurring the twelve as \"Netflix\",\n  monthly, and show it — the twelve stay as they are, the plan continues from 01/08/2026\n  for 24 months.\n\n05/07/2026  HDFC BANK EMI 884211       12,340.00  (every 5th since 05/02/2025)\n→ eighteen one-time rows, then promoted monthly, tag emi, notes \"Lender: HDFC Bank\" and\n  \"Loan account: 4211\", one per line. ends_on only if the tenure is known. Never the whole\n  loan account number: the last four digits are the most that leaves the statement.\n\n15/03/2026  HDFC ERGO HE" +
  "ALTH PREMIUM   18,450.00  (once in twelve months)\n→ one row, paid, tag insurance. A premium repeats by nature: promoted yearly as a guess,\n  name \"HDFC Ergo health (Guessed)\", notes \"Guessed: Seen once; yearly assumed from the\n  insurer's name\" — the next charge planned for 15/03/2027. Its other notes — \"Policy\n  number: …\", \"Policy name: …\", \"Document: documents/hdfc-ergo-health.pdf\" — only as far\n  as a policy document in documents/, or the owner, states them.\n\n12/01/2026  STAR HEALTH PREMIUM        9,200.00  and 12/07/2026 STAR HEALTH PREMIUM 9,200.00\n→ two rows, paid, tag insurance. Seen twice: promoted custom, custom_cycle_days 183, as a\n  guess — \"Star Health (Guessed)\", notes \"Guessed: Seen twice, half a year apart; the same\n  rhythm assumed\".\n\n01/07/2026  UPI-RAMESH K-cook         " +
  "  6,000.00  (every 1st)\n→ add_person \"Ramesh K\", as printed, never \"Ramesh\" or \"the cook\"; rows with member\n  \"Ramesh K\", then promoted monthly, tag salary, notes \"Role: cook\". Who he is to the\n  owner is the question, not whether he is in People.\n\n03/06/2026  GODADDY.COM                3,597.00  (once; the owner says it was a 3-year deal)\n→ one row, promoted custom, custom_cycle_days 1095, tag domain & hosting, notes\n  \"Registrar: GoDaddy\". The owner said so, so it is no guess. A 24-month plan writes\n  nothing more: the next renewal is three years out, and add_charges writes it the day the\n  owner asks.\n\n10/06/2026  IRCTC                      4,560.00\n→ one row, tag travel. It stays once.\n\nSPOTIFY 119.00 on the 12th, Sept 2025 through April 2026, then nothing\n→ eight rows, then promoted m" +
  "onthly with ends_on 2026-04-12: one commitment, eight charges\n  on record, nothing planned past the day it stopped.\n\n02/07/2026  APPLE.COM/BILL             0.00   then 02/08/2026 APPLE.COM/BILL 99.00\n→ two rows — the first is a charge of zero, which is what a trial is — tag subscription or\n  storage; say which you chose. Seen twice, a month apart: promoted monthly as a guess,\n  \"Apple (Guessed)\", notes \"Guessed: Seen twice, a month apart; the same rhythm assumed\".\n\n28/06/2026  CREDIT CARD PAYMENT        42,310.00   → skip (the memory holds no bill row for it;\n                                                     the card statement has the charges)\n30/06/2026  SALARY CREDIT             1,50,000.00  → one row, direction \"in\" (money in, kept apart)\n14/06/2026  ATM WDL                   10,000." +
  "00    → skip (a movement)\n```\n\nThe questions to the owner after the facts are written, in shape — one message, nothing\nabout a charge the statement already settles:\n\n> The year is in. Three things and I can finish. (1) ₹5,400 to a name I cannot place, twice\n> since March — what is it, and who is it for? (2) Hoornapriya Amba Fue is paid ₹2,000 on the\n> 7th of every month, so she is in People as printed and the payment is set to continue —\n> who is she to you? (3) Netflix, rent, the cook, Airtel and the society dues come round\n> every month, so they are set to continue; say if any is not. HDFC Ergo and Star Health are\n> set as guesses, yearly and half-yearly, with the reason in their notes: rename either to\n> keep it, or say if it is not one. Say it out loud if that is easier; rough answers " +
  "are fine.\n\nThe summary to the owner once the answers are in, in shape:\n\n> 87 charges from the statement, all recorded as paid. Thirteen of them turned out to be\n> nine commitments that repeat — Netflix, rent, the cook, Airtel, the society dues, Adobe,\n> Amazon Prime, Claude, and the two premiums, those two as guesses named so — each planned\n> 24 months ahead; Spotify\n> stopped in April and is on record that way. Twelve salary credits are in as money in,\n> kept apart from spending. Two card bill payments and three ATM withdrawals are in as\n> ordinary rows, none of them a transfer. HDFC savings now stands at ₹1,23,456 as of\n> 30 June, the statement's closing balance. Review it in the Proposals screen — keep or undo\n> it there.\n\n### Statements that arrive as Excel or CSV\n\nColumns are usually " +
  "Date, Narration/Description, Debit, Credit, Balance. Debit lines are\ncharges; Credit lines are money in, written with `direction: \"in\"`, unless they are the\nowner's own transfer. Dates come in the bank's format (DD/MM/YYYY is common in India; read the\nheader row and one known date before assuming). A narration often carries the payee in the\nmiddle of a reference string — \"UPI/123456789/NETFLIX/…\" — so match on the word, not the\nline.\n\n## The past is stored, the future is written — nothing is computed\n\nEvery charge is a row in `charges[]`, keyed by (commitment_id, date). longbook computes no\noccurrence from any rule: history is exactly the rows before today, the plan is exactly the\nrows from today on, and a commitment with no rows has no charges anywhere — not on the\ndashboard, not in a tot" +
  "al, not in a report.\n\n- A **commitment** holds only what every charge of it shares: name, `amount` (the price\n  now — what a newly planned charge is written at), currency, `billing_cycle`, category,\n  member, account, notes, details, status, and `ends_on`. Nothing about an occurrence\n  lives there. \"Renewing since\" is its earliest row. \"Next due\" is its first unpaid row on\n  or after today. \"Planned till\" is its last row.\n- A **charge** holds what is particular: `date`, `amount`, `paid`, `paid_on`, `basis`\n  (what backs it), a `note`. **A charge behind today is paid** whatever its row says: the\n  money was due and the day has passed, so every reader — the app, every tool here — reads\n  it as paid and never calls it an estimate. A row from today on is the plan, and its\n  `paid` mark is what" +
  " counts.\n- **A commitment is what still lies ahead.** A recurring one is a commitment while it is\n  active. A one-time one is a commitment until its charge is behind today or marked paid;\n  after that it is that one charge and nothing more — `commitments` leaves it out and\n  counts it, `charges` reads it. A statement of five hundred lines is five hundred charges,\n  not five hundred commitments.\n- **Nothing is written unasked.** A recurring commitment is planned when it is created —\n  `plan_months` charges from `first_charge`, or exactly `plan_count` of them, 24 months\n  when nobody says. A plan grows only when someone asks: `set_planned_count` sets how many\n  charges stand from today on (240 at most) — more are written a cycle apart after the\n  last, unpaid planned rows past the count are " +
  "unwritten, paid and hand-set rows stay;\n  `add_charges` writes named days. The app never extends a plan on its own; when one runs\n  short it says so, and `commitments` and `upcoming` name it. A one-time commitment is\n  one charge on its `date`.\n- **Changing a commitment changes what is shared.** Its `amount` reaches only charges that\n  are unpaid, planned and dated from today (or `from`) on. Nothing before today ever\n  changes because a commitment was edited, and a charge someone priced by hand, or a\n  statement proved, keeps its number.\n- **Changing a charge changes that charge.** `update_charge` changes one charge where it\n  sits — its amount, its day, its paid mark, the day the money moved, its note; with\n  `and_later` the unpaid planned rows after it follow, a cycle apart, and keep the" +
  "ir\n  amounts. A day that already holds a charge is refused. `mark_paid` remains the\n  one-field way to mark.\n- **`status` pauses, resumes or cancels:** pausing or cancelling unwrites the unpaid\n  planned charges after today and keeps what happened; back to active plans nothing —\n  `set_planned_count` continues it. A cancelled commitment's past charges still count,\n  because they did. `ends_on` is a known end: nothing is planned past it, a charge on the\n  day itself still lands. `free_trial` is a mark on the row — true while the owner calls it\n  a free trial, shown as a pill — and never a charge row.\n- Cycles: `monthly` and `yearly` step by calendar months, the day clamped into a short\n  month (the 31st becomes the 28th); `custom` steps by `custom_cycle_days`; `once` never\n  steps. The rhyt" +
  "hm only matters when a plan is written.\n- Amounts are per currency, never converted. A USD subscription and an INR rent are two\n  totals, always. A charge may state its own currency when it differs from its commitment's.\n\n## Tags — what kind of thing each row is\n\nEvery commitment, member and account can wear one tag, and `tags[]` is the vault's own\nvocabulary for them: a name and an icon. A tag says what kind of thing a row is; it never\ngives a row a shape. **No tag carries a template**: what is particular about a commitment —\na policy number, a lender, a plan — is the lines of its notes, three at most (see `Notes`\nbelow); what is particular about an account — a card's printed name, a billing day — is the\nlines of its notes too; and a member carries a name, a tag, an email and linked accou" +
  "nts,\nnothing else. A template a vault still holds on any tag is read as none.\n\nTag names match loosely — \"Insurance\", \"insurance\" and \" insurance \" are one tag — so a\nlibrary name written in any case still picks up its icon. An icon is an SF Symbol name from the\nlibrary list above — a new tag takes one of those, or none. Prefer the library; invent only\nwhen nothing fits (\"colony kitty\", \"pet care\"). A vault that grows \"Home\", \"Comms\",\n\"Househelps\", \"Social Media\" and \"AI subscription\" as its own tags is a real vault, and\nlongbook keeps those; but \"telecom\", \"salary\", \"social media\" and \"ai\" were already there\nwith icons, and an AI that knows the library saves the owner the tidy-up.\n\n`rename_tag` renames a tag in the vault's own vocabulary and carries every row wearing it,\nthe owner's tag g" +
  "roups too; `delete_tag` removes it and the rows it wore are left with no\ncategory. Both only on the owner's word — a tag the owner typed is theirs.\n\nThe library, with icons (SF Symbol names):\n\ncommitment tags:\n- food & groceries [fork.knife]\n- shopping [bag]\n- subscription [arrow.triangle.2.circlepath]\n- streaming [play.tv]\n- software [app.badge]\n- ai [sparkles]\n- social media [at]\n- telecom [antenna.radiowaves.left.and.right]\n- insurance [shield.checkered]\n- emi [building.columns]\n- rent [house]\n- utility [bolt]\n- salary [banknote]\n- education [graduationcap]\n- fees [doc.text]\n- membership [checkmark.seal]\n- domain & hosting [globe]\n- travel [airplane]\n- health & fitness [heart]\n- home [wrench.and.screwdriver]\n- vehicle [car]\n- gift [gift]\n- donation [heart.circle]\n- warranty & amc [check" +
  "mark.shield]\n- tax [percent]\n\nmember tags:\n- self [person.crop.circle]\n- family [person.2]\n- household help [person.badge.clock]\n- team [briefcase]\n- vendor [shippingbox]\n- professional [person.text.rectangle]\n- landlord [building.2]\n- tenant [key]\n- friend [hand.wave]\n\naccount tags:\n- credit card [creditcard.fill]\n- debit card [creditcard]\n- bank account [building.columns]\n- upi [qrcode]\n- wallet [wallet.pass]\n- cash [banknote]\n- brokerage [chart.line.uptrend.xyaxis]\n- loan account [doc.plaintext]\n\nA tag for a real household, from a real vault's shape: insurance is usually the biggest\ncluster (term life, health floater, car, two-wheeler, home — most yearly, some half-yearly);\nhome holds rent, society maintenance, electricity, gas, water, internet; telecom holds two\nor three phone plans; s" +
  "alary holds the cook, the cleaner, the driver; subscription, streaming,\nai, software and social media hold the digital stack; domain & hosting runs on 1–3 year\ncycles; travel and gift are mostly once.\n\n## Categorise my longbook — the rows that wear no category\n\n\"Categorise my longbook\" is one pass over every commitment that has no tag — the ones the\napp lists under \"No category\". `commitments` with status \"all\" gives every row, past and\nplanned, with its tag or none; `charges` over a wide window shows what each one has been\npaying, which is often the clue. `tags` gives the vault's own names beside the library's:\nthose first, a new one only when nothing fits.\n\nWhere the name says what the row is, write it: \"Netflix\" is streaming, \"Airtel\" is telecom,\n\"HDFC Ergo\" is insurance, \"Ramesh\" paid " +
  "on the 1st is salary. For the rest, guess from the\nname and what the row has been paying, and write your guess. One batched\n`update_commitment` pass, each with its `tag`; then show every guess in one table —\nname, date, amount, the category you chose — ask the owner to correct any that are wrong,\nand say a spoken answer is fine. Apply the corrections in one more pass, and stop: \"review it\nin Proposals\". A tag the owner names is theirs, in their words.\n\n## Reorganise my longbook — the whole book, once over\n\n\"Reorganise my longbook\" is one pass over everything, the way the owner would keep it if\nthey had an afternoon: names as they would say them, every row in a category, people and\naccounts linked, doubles merged, nothing lost. longbook's own AI runs it from the app's\nAutomations screen; th" +
  "e owner's AI runs it from the prompt; both follow this chapter, and\nevery change waits in Proposals, where the owner keeps or undoes the whole pass at once.\n\nRead everything first: `commitments` with status \"all\", `charges` over a wide window,\n`people`, `accounts`, `tags`. Then, in this order:\n\n1. **Names.** A name written the bank's way — \"NETFLIX.COM 4402\", \"ACH DEBIT HDFC ERGO\" —\n   becomes the name the owner would say: \"Netflix\", \"HDFC Ergo car insurance\". A person's\n   name stays their name. Never shorten what is already a name.\n2. **Categories.** Every commitment wears a tag: the book's own words first, then the\n   library's; a new one only when nothing fits. Two tags that mean one thing — \"Streaming\"\n   and \"OTT\" — become one, by `rename_tag` onto the one kept, which moves every wea" +
  "rer.\n   Doubt is a tag too: guess from the name and what the row has been paying, and say so in\n   the reason.\n3. **People and accounts.** A commitment paid to or for a person is linked to them,\n   `add_person` first when the book has no such person; a commitment the charges show on\n   one account is linked to that account. A card the book shows paying a bank is linked to\n   it. Never guess a person from a role or an amount: only a name the row itself carries.\n4. **Doubles.** One payee under two commitments: give both the same right name and\n   category, and say in the reason that they are one — the owner merges them in the app.\n   One charge recorded twice, the same day and amount under two commitments, both paid:\n   `remove_charges` the copy on the newer commitment. Nothing else is ever " +
  "removed.\n5. **Notes.** A note that holds a card number, a whole account number or a password is\n   rewritten without it. Otherwise notes are the owner's words; leave them.\n\nWhat a reorganisation never changes: an amount, a date, a cycle, a status, an ends_on, a\nplanned count, a balance, a charge that happened. A row already right is left alone. One\npass, batched; then stop, and say in plain words what was renamed, categorised, linked and\nmerged, and that it all waits in Proposals.\n\n## People and accounts\n\n`members[]` is who a commitment is **for** — a child's school fee, a parent's health\npremium, a team member's software seat. `accounts[]` is what it is **billed to** — a card, a\nbank account, UPI, a wallet, cash. Either can be a placeholder (`is_incomplete: true`, just\na name) until the o" +
  "wner fills it in; both `add_commitments` and `update_commitment` make\nplaceholders for a `member` or `account` name that does not exist yet; `update_account`'s\n`member` and `linked_to` need a name the memory holds. `accounts` and `people` list\nevery one with what it holds.\n`add_person` and `add_account` make a person or an account the owner named, with what is\nknown — a name already held is refused rather than doubled; `update_person` and\n`update_account` change one that exists. Name a person only when the owner did.\n\n**A star keeps one first.** The owner stars the people and accounts they look at most:\n`starred: true` lists them first on People and Accounts, and only starred ones stand in\nthe Mac sidebar. `update_person` and `update_account` take `starred` in their set; set it\nonly when t" +
  "he owner asks, never to tidy.\n\n**Hints are not people or accounts.** `hints[]` holds what setup asked: people by role\n(\"Cook\") and accounts by kind (\"Savings account\"). Use them to read statements, and add the\nreal person or account under the name a statement or the owner gives; leave the hint alone.\n\n**A person's tag is their role in the owner's finances** — landlord, household help,\nfamily, tenant, a professional they pay. Set it with `update_person` when the owner says\nit (\"Ramesh is our cook\" → household help); never guess it from a payment. The library's\nmember tags first, a new one only when nothing fits. A member carries a name, a tag, an\nemail and linked accounts — the accounts that belong to them — and nothing else: what the\nowner says about a person's role belongs in the notes of" +
  " the commitment it is about\n(\"Role: cook\" on the salary).\n\nAccounts carry identification only: display name (\"Amex 1007\"), institution (\"American\nExpress\"), `last4`, a nickname, a tag, `notes`, and the person it belongs to. What is\nparticular about an account is the lines of its notes, one per line as `Label: value`. A\ncard's **card name** is one — the product printed on it, \"Card name: Platinum Reserve\",\nnever a number. It is what to read, with the institution, when the owner asks \"any offers\non my cards?\". Write it, and any other detail, with `update_account`'s `notes`,\nrewritten as the account now stands.\n\n`details` on an account or a member is longbook's own, as on a commitment: `accounts` and\n`members` return any a row holds exactly as stored, and no write takes it —\n`update_account` " +
  "and `update_person` refuse a non-empty `card_name` or `details` by name,\nand name an empty one, as any key they do not take, under `ignored`.\n\nAn account can name its **linked account** — the account it is linked to: a card to the\nbank account that pays it, one level deep. The file keeps this under its older key,\n`parent_account_id`; `accounts` reads it as `linked_to`, and `update_account` takes\n`linked_to`. An account may also carry a `balance` and its\n`balance_date` — what it held on that day, as a statement's closing balance or the owner's\nown word; write it after importing any statement that prints one, dated the statement's\nlast day, and never compute anything from it; `accounts` and `whoami` say when the\nbalance held is older than the newest confirmed charge on that account, and\n`add" +
  "_commitments` and `add_charges` answer with a `balance_note` when the rows just\nwritten outrun it. The owner's own groups (\"credit cards\", \"debit cards\",\n\"household\", \"team\") live in `groups[]` and are labels over existing ids — never create a\nrecord to fill a group.\n\n## The documents folder — the source of past truth\n\n`documents/` beside the memory is where statements, policy PDFs, invoices and receipts\nlive. It is the permanent record the memory was built from: a statement stays there after it\nis imported, so any later question (\"what did I pay Star Health in 2025?\") can be answered\nfrom the paper, not from memory. When a row comes from a document, cite it: a line of the\ncommitment's notes names the file by its path (`Document: documents/term-policy.pdf`), and\n`_provenance[id].source` sa" +
  "ys where the write came from (`\"documents/hdfc-2026.pdf\"`).\nNever paste a document's contents into the vault; reference it.\n\nFormats: PDF that carries text, `.xlsx`, `.csv`. A scanned PDF, a `.docx`, a legacy `.xls`\nor an image is listed as unsupported with a one-line way out (\"save it as .xlsx or CSV\") —\nthe app's own importer reads `.xls`; the documents tool does not. A file still in iCloud and\nnot yet downloaded is named so and its download nudged — ask again in a moment.\n\n## Document invoices — receipts and bills in the owner's files\n\n\"Add document invoices in my longbook\" means the files, not the mail: the invoices, receipts\nand bills the owner keeps as documents. Where to look, in order: the longbook folder's\n`documents/` (`list_documents`, then `read_document` each); the owner's Doc" +
  "uments and\nDownloads folders only when your own app can read files there — Claude Desktop on a Mac\nwith file access — because no longbook tool reaches outside `documents/`; through a\nhosted door, ask the owner to attach the files to the chat, or to drop them in `documents/`.\n\nAn invoice or a receipt is one charge. Paid, at the day it names, when it says paid or that\nday has passed; `paid: false` at its due date when the due date is ahead. Name it as the\nowner would say it — \"Plumber, kitchen tap\", never the invoice number — with `basis:\n\"statement\"` (the paper is the evidence), `source: \"documents/<file>\"` and a\n`Document: documents/<file>` line in its notes; the amount, the currency and the day only\nas printed. `search` the payee first: a charge already written from a mail or a statement\n" +
  "for that amount and day is the same money, and the invoice gives it the better name and\nnote — update it, never a twin. When the payee is a commitment the memory holds, the\ninvoice is that commitment's charge: `add_charges` on it. One batch, then stop: it waits\nin Proposals.\n\n## Notes — the three details that matter\n\nA commitment's `notes` holds its three most important details, one per line, each as\n`Label: value`. There are no per-category fields; the category decides which three, not the\nshape. The owner reads the note whole on the commitment's own page. Examples by category —\npick the three the owner would look for first, and leave out any the source does not state:\n\n- insurance — Policy number; Policy name, the official name as printed; Document, the\n  policy's file name in `documents" +
  "/` (\"Document: documents/hdfc-life-policy.pdf\")\n- emi — Lender; Loan ends; Loan account, last four digits only\n- rent — Landlord; Deposit; Lease ends\n- food & groceries, shopping — Merchant; Kind; Billed through (a card's or an app's name)\n- subscription, streaming, software, ai, social media — Plan; Billed through (App Store,\n  Google Play, a card's name); Seats or profiles\n- telecom — Provider; Plan; Number, last four digits only\n- utility — Provider; Consumer number; Place (\"Home\", \"Office\")\n- salary — Role; Paid by (UPI, cash, bank transfer); Started\n- education, fees — Institution or payee; Student; Term\n- membership, health & fitness — Organisation; Membership number; Valid till\n- domain & hosting — Registrar; Domain; Auto-renew\n- travel — Destination; Dates; Booked through\n- home — " +
  "Kind; Vendor; Invoice, the file name in `documents/`\n- vehicle — Vehicle; Registration; Kind (service, insurance, fuel)\n- warranty & amc — Item; Vendor; Coverage ends\n- tax — Kind; Period; Receipt, the challan's file name in `documents/`\n- donation, gift — Organisation or recipient; Occasion or purpose; Receipt, the file name\n  in `documents/`\n- any other tag — the three the owner would look for first\n\nThe rules:\n\n1. **Three lines at most**, one detail to a line — `Policy number: 88213`, never a\n   paragraph.\n2. **Only what the source states.** When it leaves some out, ask the owner once, listing\n   the gaps, rather than guessing.\n3. **Never a secret.** No card number, CVV, expiry, PIN, password or OTP, and no whole bank\n   or loan account number — the last four digits are fine. The valida" +
  "tor refuses a save\n   carrying a card number or a credential, and names the row. A number of 13 to 19 digits\n   can read as a card number and be refused: write its last four digits, and name the\n   document that holds it in full. Dates in notes are written YYYY-MM-DD, never 03/27,\n   which can read as a card's expiry.\n4. **The present, not a log.** When the commitment changes — its price, its cycle, its\n   member, what you learned about it — rewrite the note so it describes the commitment as\n   it now stands. Never append \"updated on…\" lines: the Proposals screen keeps every version,\n   and `_provenance` keeps who wrote what and from where.\n5. **A document is named, never pasted** — by its path in `documents/`.\n\nA charge's own `note` stays a word about that one charge. `details` on a commi" +
  "tment is\nlongbook's own — `statement_line`, the words a statement printed, written when longbook\nreads a statement itself: read it, never write it.\n\nAn account's `notes` hold its details the same way, one per line as `Label: value` — on a\ncard, \"Card name: Platinum Reserve\", the product printed on it, never its number. A member\nhas no notes: a name, a tag, an email and linked accounts are all it carries.\n\nA truer start is a question for the owner, not a decision: \"this insurance has run since\n2018\" when the file's first row is 2024 means the earlier premiums were paid — the only\nquestion is whether the owner wants those years in his memory. Ask. If yes, `add_charges`\nwith `paid: true`, at the year's premium where a document says it and at today's otherwise,\nnoting which. \"Since\" is the ear" +
  "liest row, so writing them moves it.\n\nWhere your own reference belongs: `_provenance.source` for where a write came from\n(`\"gmail:18c2…\"`, `\"documents/hdfc-2026.pdf\"`), a charge's own `note` for a word about\nthat one charge. A commitment's notes hold its details, never your bookkeeping. Everything\nyou keep is in the file the owner reads.\n\n## Reading reports — drawn by longbook, read by you\n\nlongbook draws one report for every calendar month holding a **confirmed** charge, into\n`reports/<month>-<year>.json` — `july-2026.json`, `june-2026.json` — and redraws it when the\nmonth's rows change. It also draws spans of days: a trip the owner adds in the app, and any\nspan asked for with `create_report` — redrawn with the months, on the same rules.\n\nA charge is confirmed when it is dated today or ea" +
  "rlier and its `basis` is anything but\n`\"plan\"` — a statement, a mail, the owner's word, the app's own paid-tap. A row with no\n`basis` reads as `\"plan\"`, as it does everywhere else. **That one rule decides both things:\nwhich months are drawn, and what each report counts.** A month holding nothing but the plan\ndraws no report, because a report of it would describe a month nobody has confirmed; the\nmonth ahead never draws; and a transfer confirms nothing. No statement is needed — charges\nthe owner typed draw their month like any other.\n\n**A report counts confirmed charges only**, in every figure it holds: the total, the count,\n\"Money in\", both rings, the charge list, and the month it is compared against. So a month\npart-way confirmed reports the part that is confirmed and no more — a report i" +
  "s what moved,\nnot what is expected to. The rest of the month is still on the timeline and in the\ndashboard's own figures, which do count a past row whatever its basis; a report is the one\nsurface that will not.\n\n**And it says when it is short**, so you never read a light month as a cheap one. A month is\n**part-covered** when a charge in it, in the report's own currency, has passed and is not\nconfirmed. Then, and only then, the stats carry a **\"Not yet confirmed\"** stat with the count of\nthose charges — the \"Total expenses\" figure is what longbook can account for, and that stat says\nhow many charges it could not.\n\nCoverage is measured in the report's own currency, because that is what its figures are in. A\ndollar row nobody has confirmed does not make a rupee report part-covered; the dollar" +
  " month is\nits own report, with its own coverage.\n\n**`delta_pct` and `delta_is_good` are absent** whenever a change cannot honestly be drawn,\nwhich is any of three things. Read absence as \"longbook would not say\", never as a month of\nno spending, and never compute the change yourself from two reports:\n\n1. **The month is not over.** The current month's report covers its passed days only, so it\n   never carries a change: five days of September against the whole of August is a fall of\n   98% that did not happen. A month gets its change the first time it is drawn after it ends.\n2. **Either month is part-covered.** A whole month against a short one reports missing\n   evidence as thrift, and `delta_is_good` would call it good news.\n3. **The month before holds no confirmed spending in this currenc" +
  "y** — the vault's earliest\n   month, or a month whose spending was all in some other currency. There is nothing to\n   compare with, which is not the same as nothing having been spent.\n\nSay what the stats say. A report carrying \"Not yet confirmed\" is the month as far as longbook\nknows it, and the way to make it the whole month is to reconcile the month — a statement, the\nmail, or the owner marking what they recognise — after which that stat goes and, once the month\nis over, a change appears. Never present a part-covered figure as the month's spending, and\nnever fill the gap with an estimate of your own.\n\nlongbook still draws every report, and a file you put in that folder is not what the app\ndraws. `create_report` is how an AI asks it to draw one — over days, accounts, people or\ncategories " +
  "the owner chooses (\"create-a-report\"). What you do with a report is read it, say\nwhat it shows, and — when the owner wants it different — change the rows behind it, so the\nnext draw says the true thing.\n\n`reports` is the tool. With no id it lists what is there, newest first by the last day each\ncovers: the title, its `start_date` and `end_date`, its `scope`, the month when it is one,\nthe day the file was written, how many modules and how many comments each holds, and the\nshare link when one is live. With an id it returns that report whole — every module as the\napp draws it, each with its own comments.\n\nA drawn report holds these modules, in this order:\n\n1. **`stats`, \"Total expenses\"** — the stat \"Total expenses\" is the month's total, against\n   the month before when a change can be drawn " +
  "(`delta_pct`, `delta_is_good`, and\n   `delta_label` naming the month, \"vs August\"); money in another currency is a further\n   \"Total expenses\" stat in that currency, never blended in; \"Charges\" counts every row the\n   list holds, whatever its currency; \"Not yet confirmed\" appears only in a part-covered\n   month; \"Money in\" when the month had any.\n2. **`donut`, \"Accounts\"** — one slice per paying account, biggest first, with \"No account\"\n   among them when charges were billed to none.\n3. **`donut`, \"Categories\"** — one slice per tag, biggest first, \"No category\" among them.\n4. **`transactions`, \"All charges\"** — every confirmed charge in the month, biggest first,\n   uncapped. A row names its charge (`id`), its `account` and its `member` when it has\n   them. A row the owner took out of the r" +
  "eport carries `removed: true`, is listed last, and\n   is in no figure; a charge dropped for good is named in the envelope's `hidden_ids` and\n   listed nowhere.\n\nThe total and the charge list are always drawn. Both rings are drawn from the report's own\ncurrency, so a month holding no charge in that currency holds neither ring: a drawn report is\none, two or four modules, never three.\n\nOnly the report's own currency enters a ring; a slice in any other is listed beside it with\nits own sum and no share. An `Accounts` slice is an account name the owner typed and is shown\nexactly as typed; a `Categories` slice is a tag, and a screen shows it with a capital.\n\nThe envelope: `version` 2, `id` (the filename), `title` (\"July 2026\", or the name the owner\ngave — \"Goa\"), `subtitle` (\"Drawn by longbook\")," +
  " `start_date` and `end_date` (the days it\ncovers), `period` (`\"2026-07\"` only when it is one calendar month), `scope` (the `accounts`,\n`members` and `categories` it was narrowed to, lists that must all match; absent means all of\nthem, \"No account\" stands for charges billed to no account and \"No category\" for untagged\nones), `hidden_ids`, `created_at` (the day the file was written, never the days it covers),\n`currency`, `modules`. A month's id is `<month>-<year>`.\n\nA report over any other span of days has the same shape, with its own `start_date`,\n`end_date` and `scope`, and no `period`. A **trip**'s id is `trip-` with its name and first\nday — `trip-goa-2026-09-28`; a span asked for with `create_report` is named `report-` the\nsame way — `report-school-fees-2026-07-01`. When a report with ot" +
  "her days or another scope already\nholds that id, the new one takes `-2`, then `-3`; the same name, days and scope again redraws\nthe same file. longbook redraws both from their own name, days and scope whenever it redraws\nthe months. A trip still running — started with \"Start trip mode\" and not yet ended with \"End\ntrip\" — is named in `longbook.workspace.json` (`openTrip`, longbook's own), and its `end_date`\nmoves to today at each redraw until it ends. A span's `currency` is the one most of its\nconfirmed charges are in, else the home currency. The listing's order is the Reports\nscreen's: the newest last covered day first, then the newer file, then the filename; a\nreport naming no days lists last.\n\nA file older than this version may hold other module kinds — `prose`, `bars`, or a kind this\nte" +
  "xt does not name. They still read, and `reports` returns them as they are.\n\n### Comments\n\nEvery module carries a `comment`: the owner's own line, and the lines pulled back from a\nshared link when they shared the report with someone. `reports` returns them per module, one\nstring per line, in the words they were written in. Quote them; never rewrite them, never\nsummarise them into something the writer did not say. They are the one part of a report\nnobody drew.\n\n### What the owner asks for, and what to do\n\n- *\"What did I spend in July in my longbook?\"* — `reports` with the month, and read the \"Total expenses\"\n  module back. The figures are drawn from the rows; quote them, never recompute them.\n- *\"Why is July so high?\"* — the \"All charges\" module is the month, biggest first. Name the\n  charge" +
  "s that made it, not a share of a total.\n- *\"This category is wrong\"* / *\"that charge was for my mother\"* — that is the month's data,\n  not the report. Fix the row — `update_commitment`, `add_charges`, `remove_charges`,\n  `update_account`, `update_person` — and say the report redraws itself from it.\n- *\"What did Meera say?\"* — the comments on the module she left them on.\n- *\"Make a report of my Goa trip in my longbook\"* — the prompt `Make a report of my trip`, built on\n  \"create-a-report\": ask for its days, accounts and people, then one `create_report`.\n\nA report is never edited into agreement with the owner. The rows are the memory; the report\nis what the rows come to.\n\n## Month report — adding the month the owner names\n\n\"Add September month report in my longbook\" asks for September made w" +
  "hole, so that longbook\ndraws it: you write no report, and `reports` only reads. Say where you are in time first —\nthe month named, and today. Then `charges` from the month's first day to its last (and\n`reports` on the Mac, when the month is already drawn) for what is there and on what\nbasis: a passed row whose basis is \"plan\" is unconfirmed, and it is what puts a \"Not yet\nconfirmed\" stat beside the report's \"Total expenses\".\n\nAsk which bank and card statements cover the month: on the Mac, `list_documents` names\nwhat is in `documents/`; through a hosted door, ask the owner to attach them. Import them\nby \"importing-statements\" — every line a one-time row at its real date and amount, paid,\nbasis \"statement\". A row already there that the statement proves is `add_charges` on it,\npaid, basis \"st" +
  "atement\", never a twin; a charge the month held only as a plan is confirmed\nthe same way. Each statement's closing balance goes on its account — `update_account` with\n`balance` and `balance_date` the statement's last day — so the Accounts module shows the\nmonth's end rather than an older day; skip it when the statement prints none. Then say it: longbook draws the report the next time the app applies the\nchange, with a \"Not yet confirmed\" stat beside \"Total expenses\" until every passed charge in\nthe month is confirmed, and without it after.\n\n## Create a report — any days, narrowed to accounts, members or categories\n\n\"Create a report in my longbook\" asks longbook to draw a report over days the owner chooses —\na trip, a quarter, one person's spending, one card's month. longbook draws it from " +
  "the\nledger: you write no report, and you never compute or state its figures yourself.\n\nBefore you call anything, ask the owner, in one message:\n\n1. **The name** — what the report is called on the Reports screen (\"Goa\", \"Q3 household\").\n2. **The first and last day.** The first is today or earlier; 366 days at most, both counted.\n3. **Which accounts and which people.** Choosing nobody means everyone.\n4. **Categories** — only if they want to narrow by them.\n\nThen read `accounts`, `people` and `tags` for the real names, and pass each as the memory\nholds it. \"No account\" means charges billed to no account and \"No category\" untagged ones;\nany other name the memory does not hold is refused with the real names, and nothing is ever\ncreated to fit.\n\nMake ONE `create_report` call — `title`, `start_da" +
  "te`, `end_date`, and `accounts`,\n`members` or `categories` only where the owner narrowed it; on a hosted door, with an\n`idempotency_key`. Then say that longbook draws it on its Reports screen the next time the\napp is open, counting confirmed charges only as every report does, and stop. Nothing waits in\nProposals. The same name, days and scope asked again redraws the same report, so a retry is\nharmless.\n\nTo change what it says, fix the rows — the charge and commitment tools — and longbook redraws\nit. Other days or another scope is another report. Once drawn, `reports` reads it on the Mac.\nWith only the folder there is no way to ask: `requests/` is longbook's own, never written by\nhand.\n\nA trip is the same kind of report. The owner can also add one in the app, under Settings →\nTrips on the M" +
  "ac or More → Trips on iPhone, and start trip mode for one still running.\n\n## Leaving insights\n\nBeside the ledger you can keep `insights.json` — short notes about what the numbers cannot\nsay, shown inside the app on the module each note belongs to. `leave_insight` appends one;\nby hand the shape is:\n\n`{ \"version\": 1, \"insights\": [ { \"module\": \"dashboard.this_month\", \"text\": \"August runs over\nJuly — the travel booking, not a trend.\", \"created_at\": \"2026-08-07\" } ] }`\n\nAppend new entries rather than editing old ones; the last entry for a module wins. Module\nids: `dashboard.next_payment`, `dashboard.active_commitments`, `dashboard.next_30_days`,\n`dashboard.this_month`, `dashboard.by_account`, `dashboard.by_category`, plus\n`account.<account_id>` and `member.<member_id>` for one account or person" +
  ". Say what the\nnumbers cannot; never repeat them. Quote the owner's names exactly as typed. An id from a\nnewer contract than the app is kept for the version that names it — it is not an error.\n\n**There are no `reports.*` ids.** The Reports screen shows the months longbook drew and\nnothing else, so `reports.summary`, `reports.cash_flow`, `reports.spendings`,\n`reports.by_account` and `reports.top_charges` name screens that are not there; a note\nwritten on one has nowhere to appear, and longbook says so instead of hiding it. What you\nwould have said about a month belongs in the conversation, or on the dashboard module it is\nabout. Notes already written against those ids stay in `insights.json` exactly as they\nwere; longbook does not edit the owner's file to tidy its own vocabulary.\n\n## Unatte" +
  "nded runs — when no one is watching\n\nAn AI that runs on a schedule (a morning brief, a receipts sweep, a renewal watch) writes\ninto longbook without the owner in the conversation. The rules above still hold; these are\nadded because nobody is there to catch a mistake:\n\n- **Call `whoami` first, every run.** One call tells you who the connection belongs to,\n  what it may do, what is left of the rate allowance, whether a hosted copy exists, how many\n  proposals are waiting for the owner, how many FAILED to apply, and the vault's real tag\n  names. If it says `can_write: false`, read `how_to_enable`, and say that sentence to the\n  owner rather than trying anyway. `failed_to_apply` lists the newest suggestions the app\n  could not carry out (up to 20; `failed_to_apply_count` is the total), each wi" +
  "th its\n  reason — usually a name the memory does not hold. Fix the cause with the real names and\n  file again under the same `idempotency_key`, so the corrected proposal replaces the\n  failed one; never resend the same payload.\n- **Both doors write; they land differently.** On the owner's own machine the local server\n  writes the file, and the batch appears in Proposals at once. From the cloud — either signed\n  in as the owner, or carrying a key with `memory:propose` — a write is a **proposal**:\n  nothing out there touches the memory, and the owner's app applies it, on their Mac or\n  their iPhone, whichever they open next, where it stands in Proposals to keep or undo like\n  any other outside edit. `write_mode` in whoami says which door you are at. The honest\n  sentence to the owner is \"it " +
  "will be waiting in Proposals the next time you open longbook\",\n  never \"done\".\n- **If the write tools refuse with `insufficient_scope`, read `how_to_enable` and say it.**\n  Suggestions from a signed-in AI are off until the owner turns them on (\"Let signed-in AIs\n  suggest changes\", on the app's Automations screen); a key needs \"Read and suggest changes\".\n  The tools are listed either way, so you can always tell the owner exactly what to switch on.\n- **Idempotency is yours to keep, and the door helps.** Send an `idempotency_key` on every\n  hosted write, built from the SOURCE and the WRITE — not from the date:\n  `{source id}:{tool}:{short hash of the payload}`, e.g. `18c2a:add_commitments:9f3c1d`.\n  A date in the key is wrong in both directions: two writes drawn from one email collide\n  insi" +
  "de a single run and the second is silently swallowed, and tomorrow's date makes a\n  fresh key for the same email, filing a real duplicate. A repeat answers\n  `created: false` with the same entry id: that is success, not an error; do not send it\n  again a third way. The promise holds for ninety days — a suggestion is deleted ninety\n  days after it was filed, and a key that old files afresh. Where no source id exists, `search` for the name and check `notes`\n  and `_provenance.source` before adding.\n- **Cite the source on every write**: pass `source` (`\"gmail:18c2…\"`,\n  `\"documents/icici-aug.pdf\"`). It becomes `_provenance.source`, and the owner reads it in\n  the diff beside your key's name.\n- **Read the error code, not the sentence.** Every refusal carries one: `rate_limited`\n  (wait `retry_" +
  "after` seconds, then continue), `validation_failed` (fix that row or drop\n  it — never retry the same payload), `insufficient_scope` / `key_revoked` / `key_expired`\n  / `entitlement_required` (stop and tell the owner; nothing you retry will help — the hosted\n  copy needs longbook Cloud, and on a Mac the same work is free through the local server),\n  `conflict` or `upstream_error` (try once more later). `advice` says \"retry\" or \"stop\"\n  in as many words.\n- **One batch, one report, silence.** Write once, leave a short `leave_insight` if it\n  helps, and end. Never loop write-check-rewrite.\n- **Tell the owner, never decide for them.** An unattended run adds `once` rows and\n  proposes promotions in its note; it does not cancel, does not promote, does not change\n  amounts on its own authority un" +
  "less the source is a price-change notice from the payee\n  itself. The one plan it extends: when it marks a charge paid and no charge is written\n  after it, it writes the next one — one cycle on, `add_charges` at the commitment's\n  price — so the commitment keeps a next due. Say so in the report.\n- **The key lives in a credential store, never in a file or the prompt.** On a Mac the app\n  offers \"Save to keychain\" the one time the key is shown; a run on that machine then reads\n  it without the key ever touching disk:\n  `security find-generic-password -s \"longbook access key\" -w`\n  (the first read asks once — choose Always Allow). Hand it to an MCP config through the\n  environment, not by pasting: `export LONGBOOK_KEY=\"$(security find-generic-password -s\n  'longbook access key' -w)\"` and then" +
  " `\"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${LONGBOOK_KEY}\"` in the\n  connector config. In a cloud scheduler, use its own secret store the same way. Keys are\n  named, scoped and revoked on the app's Automations screen; a revoked key stops at once,\n  and an expiring one is reported by `whoami` before it bites.\n\nA morning brief, end to end: `whoami` → `upcoming` (30 days) and `next_payment` for the\nlines the owner wants read out → for each receipt or notice in the mail: `search` the payee,\nthen `add_commitments` (or `update_commitment` for a price change, `mark_paid` for a\n\"payment received\", plus the next cycle's charge when the plan ended with it) with `source`\nand `idempotency_key` → one `leave_insight` if something is worth saying → report to the\nowner what was filed and that it is waiting in Proposals." +
  "\n\n## The daily email round — a scheduled look at the owner's mail\n\nThe owner may ask you to \"set yourself up for a daily longbook email round\". It is one\nscheduled task: each morning you read the last day of their mail for money that moved, or\nis about to, and write what you find into longbook. Everything in \"Unattended runs\" holds;\nthis chapter is the method.\n\n**Setting it up, once.**\n\n1. Call `whoami`. If `can_write` is false, say its `how_to_enable` sentence and stop there.\n2. Check that you can read the owner's mail. If you cannot, say so: the round needs a mail\n   connector.\n3. Create a scheduled task that runs every day at 06:00 in the owner's time zone. Its\n   instructions: call `read_guide` with \"daily-email-round\" on the longbook connector, then\n   do \"Each run\" as that chapter sa" +
  "ys — so every morning reads the method as it stands that\n   day. If your app has no scheduled tasks, say so, and offer to run the round whenever the\n   owner asks for it.\n4. Run the first round now, so the owner sees in Proposals what a morning looks like.\n\n**One month, on request.** \"Add email invoices in my longbook\" is the same reading, once,\nover the calendar month so far — no scheduled task. `whoami`, then steps 2 to 7 of \"Each\nrun\", with the search window the month's first day to today, taking the kinds of mail below\nas they stand, a bill due ahead included at its due date. Report once and stop.\n\n**Each run.**\n\n1. `whoami` first. Anything under `failed_to_apply` is earlier work that did not land: fix\n   it with the real names and file it again under the same `idempotency_key`.\n2. Sea" +
  "rch the mail since the last round — the last 24 hours, or 36 to be safe — for the\n   kinds of mail listed below.\n3. Read the memory before you write: `accounts` gives every account with its type, its\n   notes (a card's name among them), last four digits and next unpaid charge, and\n   `people` every person with their tag; `commitments` gives what still lies ahead with\n   its price, cycle, category, account and person; `charges` with `from` set to the\n   first day you are looking back over gives what is already written, card bills among\n   them. A mail whose amount and day are already there was written by an earlier round, or\n   by a bank message: skip it. This look is what stops a twin; an idempotency key only\n   protects a retry of the same call.\n4. The fields longbook takes are in each wr" +
  "ite tool's input schema and in the chapter\n   \"shape\": a name, an amount with its currency, a date, `billing_cycle`, `paid`, `basis`,\n   `direction` for money in and for a transfer, and, when the mail says so, a category, an\n   `account`, a `member` and a short note.\n5. From each mail draw exactly those. The amount and its currency. The day the money moved,\n   which is not always the day the mail arrived. Who it went to or came from, as a plain\n   name: \"Corner Grocery\", never the payment handle, never \"UPI transfer\". The account in\n   the memory whose name carries the last four digits the mail shows. A category from\n   `tags`, the library's before a new one. A person only when the memory holds them.\n6. Write each mail once, in a call of its own, so it carries its own\n   `source: \"gmail:<m" +
  "essage id>\"` and an `idempotency_key` built from that id. `search` the\n   payee first. Money that belongs to a commitment the memory holds goes on it with\n   `add_charges`: the open charge's own date, `paid: true`, the amount, `paid_on` and\n   `basis: \"email\"` — a row already there for that day is updated in place — and a new date\n   only when no charge is open. Anything else is an `add_commitments` row of its own,\n   `basis: \"email\"`, paid or unpaid as its kind below says.\n7. End with one short report: how many rows, which mails you skipped and why in a line,\n   what needs the owner's word (a row that looks recurring, an account the memory does not\n   hold), and that it is waiting in Proposals. Then stop.\n\n**The kinds of mail, and what each becomes.**\n\n- **A bank's debit alert** — UPI, ca" +
  "rd, net banking: \"Rs.250.00 is debited from your account\n  ending 1234 towards VPA shop@bank (SHOP NAME) on 05-08-26\". A charge: a one-time row at\n  its real date and amount, paid. The name is the shop or person the mail names, the words\n  in brackets after the handle. When the payee is a commitment the memory holds — a\n  subscription, a premium, a salary the owner pays — the alert is that commitment's charge,\n  never a twin beside it.\n- **A card alert** — \"Thank you for using your Debit Card ending 1234 for Rs. 1,499.00 at\n  MERCHANT on 07-08-2026\". The same.\n- **An autopay or e-mandate chain** — a consent request with an OTP, \"consent received\",\n  \"deducted from your account\", \"successfully paid\": four mails, one payment. Write it\n  once, from the mail that says the money left. A consent" +
  " request, an OTP and a pre-debit\n  notice are not charges; when a pre-debit notice names a new amount for a commitment the\n  memory holds, say so in the report.\n- **A receipt or an invoice from the seller** — a subscription receipt, a service invoice,\n  a ride, a food order. Usually the same money a bank alert already reported: match on the\n  amount and the day, write it once, and let the receipt give the better name, the category\n  and the note. A receipt paid in cash has no bank alert and is a charge of its own.\n- **A bill that is due** — \"payment is due for your service, amount 1,800.00\". When the\n  memory holds the commitment, the bill is its next charge: check that charge's date and\n  amount, and add nothing. When it does not, a one-time row, `paid: false`, at its due date;\n  when its" +
  " payment shows in a later round, that row is the one to mark paid.\n- **Money in** — salary, a refund, interest, cashback, a dividend, once the bank says it was\n  credited: a one-time row with `direction: \"in\"`, paid. A notice that a refund is on its\n  way is not the credit.\n- **An insurance or renewal notice** — when the memory holds the commitment, check its next\n  charge's date and amount against the notice; correct the charge only when the notice comes\n  from the payee itself, and otherwise say what differs in your report. When the memory does\n  not hold the commitment, add a one-time row, unpaid, at the due date, and propose the cycle\n  in your report.\n- **A credit card statement is ready** — ONE one-time row named for the card and the month\n  it falls due, \"<Card> bill, <month> <year>" +
  "\" (\"Coral card bill, October 2026\"),\n  `direction: \"transfer\"`, `paid: false`, at the due date, the amount the total due,\n  `account` the card when the memory holds it, `basis: \"email\"`. The month is in the name\n  because a card has a bill every month: each is its own row, and a name two rows share\n  cannot address either. Never spending: every spend on that card is a charge already, and\n  the bill that pays them is a movement between the owner's own accounts — a transfer is\n  shown and counted in nothing. A bill already written for that month is corrected in\n  place, never doubled. Import the statement's own lines, by \"importing-statements\", only\n  when the owner has asked for card statements to be imported.\n- **A card bill payment** — it settles the transfer row for that month: find it w" +
  "ith\n  `search`, and address it by id. `add_charges` on that row, its own date,\n  `paid: true`, `paid_on` the day the money moved, the amount paid, `basis: \"email\"` —\n  updated in place. Skip it only when the memory holds no such row.\n- **A wallet top-up, a transfer to the owner's own account, an investment bought** —\n  movements. Skip them, unless the memory holds the payment as a commitment of its own — a\n  monthly investment plan, a standing transfer — and then it is that commitment's charge.\n- **Skip entirely** — OTPs, sign-in alerts, offers and newsletters, brokerage and mutual\n  fund statements, margin and portfolio reports, tax intimations, forex mark-up notices\n  (the fee arrives as its own debit), and anything that names no amount.\n\nThe last four digits of a card or an account are " +
  "for matching only. Never write a card\nnumber, an account number, an OTP or a tax id anywhere.\n\n## The shape\n\n- `app` — `\"longbook\"`.\n- `version` — the file format, currently 3. Not a revision counter; leave it alone.\n- `commitments[]` — the only required list, and only what every charge shares: name,\n  amount (the price now), currency, billing_cycle (monthly | yearly | custom | once — shown\n  as Commitment Type), status, free_trial (true while the owner marks it one), tag, notes\n  (its three details that matter), details (longbook's own statement line — read, never\n  written), ends_on, direction (in | out | transfer — only \"out\", or none, is spending),\n  and two foreign keys: member_id,\n  account_id. Nothing about an occurrence lives here.\n- `charges[]` — every charge, past and planned, on" +
  "e row per commitment per due date:\n  commitment_id, date, amount, paid, and optionally currency, paid_on, basis (statement |\n  user | mark_paid | plan | email), note, written_at. The only place an occurrence exists.\n- `members[]`, `accounts[]` — who a commitment is for, and what it is billed to. The `people`\n  tool lists the first; the file keeps its older key. Both carry\n  a tag, details — longbook's own, read as stored, never written — and starred (true when\n  the owner starred one to keep it first; set it only when they ask); an account also notes\n  (its details, one per line, a card's name among them), member_id (the person it belongs\n  to) and parent_account_id (its linked account).\n- `tags[]` — the vocabulary: kind (commitment | member | account), name and icon. No tag\n  carries a te" +
  "mplate.\n- `groups[]` — optional; the owner's own labels over what already exists, never new records.\n  An \"account\" group lists account_ids, a \"member\" group member_ids, a \"tag\" group tag_keys —\n  one named \"business\" holding tag_keys groups those tags wherever longbook totals spending.\n  A tag belongs to at most one group; anything ungrouped is simply ungrouped, not \"other\".\n- `to_fill_in[]` — optional; commitments the owner named at setup or ticked from a\n  statement, and nobody has filled in: id, name, group, tag. Not commitments. Find each\n  one's amount and day, write it with `add_commitments` under the same name, and the entry\n  goes; ask the owner for what you cannot find.\n- `hints[]` — optional; people and accounts the owner picked at setup by role or kind: id,\n  name, kind (member" +
  " | account), tag. Not members or accounts, and nothing points at one. Read\n  statements with them in mind; add the person or account under the name a statement or the\n  owner gives, and leave the hint alone.\n- `_provenance` — optional map keyed by record id: who last edited it, when, from what source.\n  longbook adds `reviewed_by`/`reviewed_at` when the owner keeps your change and keeps an\n  entry for a row it removed; leave both alone and replace the whole entry when you edit a row.\n\nNeighbours in the folder: `documents/` (statements and source documents — the owner's and\nyours to add to), `reports/` (reports, JSON — one per calendar month, named for it, plus\ntrips and the spans asked for with `create_report`; all drawn by longbook and read with\n`reports`), `requests/` (longbook's own: wh" +
  "at an AI asked of it through `create_report` on\nthis machine, waiting for the app, which carries each out and removes it — never write or\nedit it by hand),\n`insights.json` (your notes), `library.json` (the tag library this text lists),\n`longbook.schema.json` (the formal schema),\n`longbook.workspace.json` (display preferences — longbook's own, never edit) and\n`.longbook/` (change history — never edit; editing history would defeat the owner's ability\nto review and undo changes, yours included).\n\n## The past is evidence\n\nA charge row is the truth about one occurrence, and `basis` says what backs it. Best\nfirst: `\"statement\"` and `\"email\"` — a document says so; `\"user\"` — the owner said so,\nor priced it by hand; `\"mark_paid\"` — the app's own paid-tap; `\"plan\"` — written ahead\nat the commitment" +
  "'s price, confirmed by nobody yet. Anything but `\"plan\"` is a fact, and\nnothing reprices a fact.\n\n- The owner's word is the starting point, not the last word. Write what they tell you with\n  `basis: \"user\"`.\n- A document is better evidence. When a bank statement or an email arrives, reconcile against\n  it: correct the amount AND the date (`paid_on`), set `basis: \"statement\"` or `\"email\"`,\n  and point `_provenance.source` at the document in `documents/`. `add_charges` does this\n  on a row that exists — a row already there for that day is updated in place; a row that\n  does not is written. `mark_paid` carries no basis, so it is for the owner's word alone.\n- Never replace a statement- or email-backed figure with the owner's recollection. Evidence\n  does not degrade.\n- One row per occurrence, " +
  "ever. Correcting is not a second row beside the first: change the\n  row that exists and let `basis` say where the number now comes from.\n- `basis: \"mark_paid\"` is longbook's own paid-tap. Never write it yourselves.\n- A row that has passed and is still `\"plan\"` is unconfirmed, not wrong. Leave it, mark it\n  when the owner says it was paid, or correct it when a statement arrives.\n\nThe future is the plan as written, and only as far as it was written. Never write a charge\nyou were not asked for, and never remove a paid one without saying so.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- Foreign keys must resolve — a dangling member_id or account_id blocks the save.\n- Amounts are finite and non-negative; dates are real calendar dates.\n- `name_key` (trimmed + lowercased name) is the dedup key for members, accounts and tags" +
  ".\n- Details values are scalars or flat lists — never nested objects.\n- `custom_cycle_days` only with `billing_cycle: \"custom\"`. One charge per commitment\n  per date. A one-time commitment has exactly one.\n- An invalid file on disk is preserved and reported, never silently overwritten.\n\n## Rules for anything that writes\n\n1. Preserve ids — they are UUIDs and they are load-bearing.\n2. Never convert or blend currencies. Totals are per-currency, always.\n3. Never write a charge nobody asked for; plan as far as the owner said, and say how far.\n4. Sign your work in _provenance; cite the document a row came from.\n5. Prefer cancelling over deleting — a cancelled commitment keeps every charge that\n   happened and drops only the plan ahead. `delete_commitment` removes a commitment and\n   every charge " +
  "it ever had, and is for the owner's own sentence \"delete it\", never for\n   tidying.\n6. Never write a full card number, CVV, expiry, PIN, or credential anywhere in this file.\n7. Keep source documents in documents/ and reference them; never paste their contents.\n8. Dedupe before adding; once when in doubt; one batch, then stop for review.\n   Names are read on one-line cards: a commitment's name is whole up to about 28\n   characters, an account's or a person's up to about 20, and a longer one is cut with an\n   ellipsis on the phone. Write names the owner would say, short enough to be read whole.\n9. longbook drops a malformed entry alone — a charge that fails the schema vanishes from\n   every screen while the rest of the vault keeps working. If something you wrote is not\n   showing up, do not " +
  "rewrite it blind: validate this file against `longbook.schema.json`\n   and check the vault's shape first.\n10. Read before you write. Every `read_guide` answer carries a `guide_key`, and so does a\n   prompt's own message; pass it with every write the procedure makes. A write without one,\n   or with one from another rulebook, is answered with a `guide_warning` naming what to\n   read; from the next rulebook it is refused. Charges with basis \"statement\" take the key\n   of `importing-statements`, or of a prompt built on it.\n\nThe formal schema: `longbook.schema.json`, right beside this file. The seeded tag library\nwith icons: `library.json`, beside it too. Both are served by the longbook MCP servers as\nresources, and `read_guide` returns any chapter of this text by name. The owner's prompts:\n`pr" +
  "ompts.json`, beside them, the same list the `Prompts` chapter holds.\n"
